</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span> Both the Planner and the Critic are initialized from the Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct model. In the SFT stage, the Planner and the Critic are both trained for two epochs, using a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> and a batch size of 32. Then, the Critic is trained for one epoch of RL. For the RL stage, we set the learning rate to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, the global batch size to 16, the rollout number (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>) to 8, and apply a KL divergence penalty with a coefficient of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Throughout the training, the maximum image resolution is capped at <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024\times 1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1024</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024\times 1024</annotation></semantics></math>. The entire pipeline takes approximately 50 hours on eight H800 GPUs. During inference, we integrate InterleaveThinker with three distinct models to evaluate different aspects of our approach and set the maximum refinement iteration <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{max}</annotation></semantics></math> for each step to 5. We use FLUX.2-klein-9B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labs (2025)</a></cite> for in-domain evaluation and Qwen-Image-Edit-2511 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025a)</a></cite> to assess generalization capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks.</span> To systematically evaluate the capabilities of our multi-agent InterleaveThinker, we test it on two interleaved benchmarks: UEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2026a)</a></cite> and CoMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025a)</a></cite> (Tasks 3 and 4). Specifically, UEval assesses text-to-interleaved output generation, while task3 of CoMM measures interleaved input-output performance. Furthermore, we validate our method on reasoning-based benchmarks, utilizing WISE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niu et al. (2025)</a></cite> for image generation and RISE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2025)</a></cite> for image editing.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13679v1/per1.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:333/315;" width="333" height="315" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison with Emu3.5 and Nano Banana Pro in pure-text input interleaved generation</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
